{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) as a non-inductive proxy for preference, but this smuggles in induction because selecting the theory 'closer to the truth' based on past survival implicitly assumes that past performance is a reliable guide to future truth-content. Popper explicitly rejected the idea that surviving tests makes a theory more likely to be true or closer to the truth in any predictive sense; by framing the preference as a rational choice based on demonstrated robustness indicating 'fit,' the target reintroduces the very inductive principle (the uniformity of nature/theory behavior) that Popper sought to eliminate, failing to distinguish between a purely negative elimination of error and a positive heuristic for truth-approximation.","premise":"Popper rejected the notion that corroboration implies increased probability or truth-likeness, yet the target argues scientists prefer theories because they are 'closer to the truth' based on past survival.","successor_question":"Can a preference rule based on 'survivability' be formulated without implicitly assuming that the mechanisms which allowed a theory to survive in the past will continue to operate in the future?","target":"b854f658248e1c9b4c0c679104b34a989765ec17fcef37e0eff18837fc9704f8"}]}